sec.p:
Compared with participants <75 years of age, those ≥75 years (n=4023, 40%) more often reported living alone (33% versus 21%), having hypertension (48% versus 40%), and polypharmacy (taking ≥5 medications) (23% versus 17%) (Table 2). Those ≥75 years of age reported lower quality of life than those <75 years (median Short‐Form Six‐Dimension: 0.70 versus 0.74) (score closer to 0 indicating better quality of life
29
). Compared with men, women more often reported living alone (33% versus 18%), experiencing muscle symptoms (51% versus 41%), polypharmacy (25% versus 15%), not completing tertiary education (51% versus 41%), no current alcohol use (21% versus 14%), and never smoking (62% versus 47%) (Table S4). Those living in regional/rural areas had similar characteristics as those living in metropolitan areas. However, the prevalence of hypertension and polypharmacy was slightly greater in those living in regional/rural areas than in metropolitan areas (hypertension: 46% versus 41%; polypharmacy: 23% versus 18%) (Table S5).
